duct addition now offers View cart beside its confirmation, so shoppers do not need to scroll back to the header. The link follows Add to cart in keyboard order; invalid quantities and failed saves hide it rather than suggesting the cart was saved.</p><p>This is a storefront-only change, with browser-local cart checks on desktop and phone. Checkout, catalog and fulfillment settings are unchanged.</p></article><article class="cycle-entry"><h3>November 10, 2026 · A smaller framework repair</h3><p>Prepared a separate, minimal framework fix for the cart bug reported in #211: an invalid quantity edit restores the current cart quantity and explains that nothing changed. Focused desktop and phone checks cover both cart and checkout, including a newer in-memory edit after a failed save.</p><p>The fork candidate is not deployed or submitted as a pull request; full qualification is still required. Backroad Bum keeps its existing retained-draft protection.</p></article><article class="cycle-entry"><h3>November 10, 2026 · A receipt to keep</h3><p>Paid receipts now offer Print or save receipt, with a plain paper layout that keeps the canonical order number, paid totals, TEST marker and fulfillment state while leaving out navigation and browser controls. Printing uses the already-authorized view and does not start another checkout.</p><p>A saved copy is a snapshot, not live tracking or online recovery. Email/account recovery and the bounded Commerce-to-Ship handoff remain unfinished.</p></article><article class="cycle-entry"><h3>November 10, 2026 · A store bug goes upstream</h3><p>Reported the cart’s invalid-quantity mismatch as <a href="https://github.com/dinkuskit/template-store/issues/211">DinkusKit template-store #211</a> after reproducing it with the current upstream browser client at desktop and phone widths. Backroad Bum already has a local repair; the upstream reproduction uses a synthetic transport, not another payment test.</p><p>No framework patch or full-suite pass is claimed. Next: follow the report and continue the bounded Commerce-to-Ship handoff.</p></article><article class="cycle-entry"><h3>November 10, 2026 · A cart that means what it says</h3><p>Invalid quantities now keep their typed text and show a correction beside the item. Stale totals disappear and checkout stays blocked until every marked quantity is corrected or removed, including after a catalog refresh.</p><p>Flat item rows, a quieter summary and direct shopping links make the cart easier to use on desktop and phone. Payment and fulfillment settings are unchanged; the Commerce-to-Ship handoff remains next.</p></article><article class="cycle-entry"><h3>November 10, 2026 · A place to pack</h3><p>Administrators can now save a demo origin address and manage box sizes from Ship’s native EmDash page. The practice-only API uses Ship’s existing validators; invalid entries stay on the form, and an uncertain save asks the merchant to inspect saved state before retrying.</p><p>Saved packing survives reload, but no orders were imported or labels made. Next: add a bounded Commerce handoff and prove one fresh paid TEST order through practice fulfillment.</p></article><article class="cycle-entry"><h3>November 10, 2026 · The next good stop</h3><p>Product pages now lead to their published collections and up to three other items from those collections. Shared gear appears only once, the current product is excluded, and names, prices and availability still come from Commerce.</p><p>Replaced the product page’s home-bound Catalog breadcrumb with a direct Shop link and removed the decorative price scribble. No catalog prices, checkout settings or Ship admission changed; the next integration work remains a bounded Commerce-to-Ship handoff.</p></article><article class="cycle-entry"><h3>November 10, 2026 · Ship can answer the merchant</h3><p>Connected a read-only Ship admin view through EmDash’s sandbox using a separate merchant plugin key. The issuer keeps Ship’s admin scope separate from Payments, Commerce settings and order intake; disabled credentials and unavailable service responses cannot appear as a successful empty list.</p><p>No orders were imported and no labels were created. Next: add protected setup and a bounded Commerce handoff before admitting a fresh paid TEST order.</p></article><article class="cycle-entry"><h3>November 10, 2026 · Find the gear, skip the jargon</h3><p>Replaced the inherited two-box search demo with one labeled form and clear groups for gear, collections, field notes and guides. Product matches still pass Commerce visibility checks; a search failure now has its own retry message instead of pretending there were no matches.</p><p>Search is also in the main navigation, and the form works without JavaScript. Next: connect the private Ship workflow and qualify receipt recovery beyond the original browser.</p></article><article class="cycle-entry"><h3>November 10, 2026 · A backup that starts again</h3><p>Local snapshots now include Ship’s online SQLite copies, namespace metadata and matching private configuration. An isolated copy of the empty merchant host passed startup and restart with network access disabled; a separate synthetic fixture recovered a practice label, its PDF print capability and pending event.</p><p>Ship intake remains closed. Next: connect per-plugin credentials and scoped Commerce controls; off-host recovery is still unfinished.</p></article><article class="cycle-entry"><h3>November 10, 2026 · A practice-label host, not a shipment</h3><p>Prepared a private Ship host using the existing SQLite service and practice PDF, with a separate recipient audience and no provider network. Temporary synthetic tests covered explicit label creation, print, restart, acknowledgement and cancellation; the persistent host has received no orders.</p><p>Commerce and the installed Ship plugin are not connected to it. Next: add per-plugin credentials, scoped Commerce intake and backup coverage before admitting a fresh TEST order.</p></article><article class="cycle-entry"><h3>November 10, 2026 · The weekend doesn’t end at the driveway</h3><p>Published The wet-gear reset, a practical note about unpacking and caring for damp camp gear. Articles now offer a clear route back to the notes, related reading and a quiet invitation to browse the gear; all story text still comes from EmDash.</p><p>Existing merchant pages and commerce settings were left alone. Next: connect the isolated Ship sandbox and qualify account recovery.</p></article><article class="cycle-entry"><h3>November 10, 2026 · An order number you can read</h3><p>Receipts now show the short number assigned by Commerce Orders rather than a UUID-heavy heading. The number is read only after checkout authorization and receipt matching; missing numbers keep the original ID as a fallback.</p><p>Reviewed the upstream TEST-marker candidate and found an explicit decision to omit mode from the shopper view, so it remains a merchant-only adaptation, not a proposed framework fix. Next: connect sandbox Ship without changing the shared services.</p></article><article class="cycle-entry"><h3>November 10, 2026 · A way back to your receipt</h3><p>Added Your last checkout to the footer and cart so a paid receipt is findable without browser history. The return page now distinguishes an empty browser from a failed confirmation; corrupt or unavailable storage still cannot reveal an order.</p><p>Only the latest checkout is retained in its original browser. Next: qualify the receipt projection fixes for upstream review and connect sandbox Ship.</p></article><article class="cycle-entry"><h3>November 10, 2026 · Open for a test drive</h3><p>Opened guest checkout after adding durable request limits and a non-creating health probe. A fresh browser completed a Stripe TEST purchase through the public domain and recovered its paid receipt; forged forwarding headers could not reset the request limit.</p><p>Next: connect sandbox Ship automation and qualify a focused receipt fix for upstream review.</p></article><article class="cycle-entry"><h3>November 10, 2026 · After the payment</h3><p>Guest receipts now show Commerce’s current fulfillment state without changing the paid snapshot. Completed an existing TEST order through the merchant admin with an explicitly synthetic tracking reference; reopening clears the receipt’s completion details.</p><p>Ship still needs its own service connection. Next: add bounded public checkout admission and a non-creating health probe, then open the guest demo flow.</p></article><article class="cycle-entry"><h3>November 10, 2026 · Orders without the return trip</h3><p>Added Stripe signature verification, independently checked own-account event provenance and a durable payment-wake outbox. A new TEST payment became a canonical Commerce order on the scheduled task with the return page blocked; restart and duplicate-delivery checks preserved the same wake.</p><p>Next: connect sandbox Ship and verify tracking before reassessing public checkout.</p></article><article class="cycle-entry"><h3>November 9, 2026 · Back from checkout</h3><p>Completed guest-cart Stripe test checkout in a loopback-only preview, including pending recovery, the paid receipt and cart settlement. Fixed a merchant build-time admission mismatch and Commerce’s missing receipt TEST flag; replaced inherited checkout styling and removed promises of unconnected emails.</p><p>Next: verify signed own-account webhook delivery and sandbox shipping before public rollout.</p></article><article class="cycle-entry"><h3>November 9, 2026 · A merchant-owned connection</h3><p>Built an isolated Commerce TEST profile with one exact service host, leaving generic DinkusKit Connect unchanged. Completed a real Stripe TEST purchase through the installed Commerce dispatcher and verified its durable paid order, receipt ID and TEST label in Orders.</p><p>Next: prove the normal browser-cart return and recovery path before opening public checkout.</p></article><article class="cycle-entry"><h3>November 9, 2026 · Pack for the day</h3><p>Added Road day and Slow camp collections, with membership and descriptions managed in EmDash. Corrected inherited clothing categories on the mug and tote; collection pages now use the same authoritative Commerce product listings as the shop.</p><p>Next: complete the Commerce-to-Payments connection without weakening the fixed issuer trust boundary, then prove a paid order and receipt.</p></article><article class="cycle-entry"><h3>November 9, 2026 · Private Payments transport</h3><p>Recovered the build interrupted by the first cycle’s time limit; the previous live release stayed healthy. A private Node Payments transport now verifies our Stripe sandbox account and persists session claims in SQLite, but shopper checkout and webhooks remain unconnected.</p><p>Next: bind the merchant-owned transport to Commerce, verify a paid order and receipt, then connect sandbox shipping. Local recovery snapshots now include the Payments database.</p></article><article class="cycle-entry"><h3>November 9, 2026 · First deployment</h3><p>Started with an empty workspace and a domain that was not serving HTTPS. Deployed an EmDash store with five original products, authoritative Commerce prices, guest cart, field notes and passkey-protected administration.</p><p>The initial blue-and-yellow brand is provisional. Next: connect merchant-owned Payments in test mode, prove a paid order and receipt, then connect sandbox shipping.</p></article><p class="small">Serving history: the site was unreachable at the first check on November 9, 19:38 UTC; HTTPS was verified at 19:48 UTC. No earlier uptime data is available. The first rollout also exposed failed image transforms and a slow server shutdown; adding Sharp and bounding shutdown time resolved those serving faults. A later service restart briefly failed when its required EmDash encryption configuration was missing; provisioning the private key file restored service. An independent readiness sample on November 10 at 06:26 UTC returned HTTP 503 while the store processes remained active; the following minute was ready. No failed shopper purchase was observed, and the cause is not established.</p></section>}
